## H3. API Strategy — WPGraphQL (recommended) vs WC REST API

  THIS PROJECT USES: WPGraphQL + WooGraphQL (GraphQL approach)

  WHY GRAPHQL over REST:
  - Fetch only the exact fields needed (faster, smaller payloads)
  - One request = product + variations + images + categories (REST = multiple requests)
  - No over-fetching — product card needs 5 fields, REST returns 80+ fields
  - Better for shop listing pages (46 products, each with variants)
  - embodypeptides.com (reference site) likely uses the same approach

  REQUIRED WORDPRESS PLUGINS (must be installed on backend):
  ┌─────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Plugin                      │ Purpose                                     │
  ├─────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ WPGraphQL                   │ GraphQL endpoint at /graphql                │
  │ WPGraphQL for WooCommerce   │ Adds WC types (Product, Variation, Cart)    │
  │   (wp-graphql-woocommerce)  │ to the GraphQL schema                       │
  └─────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  CHECK IF INSTALLED:
    Visit: http://localhost.elixerpeptides-backend.com/graphql
    If returns JSON with "data" key → WPGraphQL is active
    If 404 → plugin not installed, install first

  INSTALL (if missing):
    WP Admin → Plugins → Add New → search "WPGraphQL" → install both plugins above

## H4. GraphQL Client Setup in Next.js

  Use: plain fetch() with POST to /graphql — no extra library needed (Next.js fetch = cached)
  Do NOT use: Apollo Client, urql, graphql-request for server components
  Why: Next.js built-in fetch has ISR/caching built-in. Adding Apollo = extra bundle size.

  PATTERN for server components (src/lib/graphql.ts):

    const query = async (gql: string, variables = {}) => {
      const res = await fetch(process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_GRAPHQL_URL!, {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
        body: JSON.stringify({ query: gql, variables }),
        next: { revalidate: 3600 }, // ISR — rebuild every 1 hour
      });
      const json = await res.json();
      if (json.errors) throw new Error(json.errors[0].message);
      return json.data;
    };

  For MUTATIONS (cart, auth) → use from client components with no cache:
    fetch(url, { method: "POST", cache: "no-store", ... })

## H5. WC REST API — when to use it (fallback / specific cases)

  Use WC REST API (not GraphQL) for:
  - Applying coupons to Woo cart (before Tagada redirect — §A7)
  - Admin-only operations (no WPGraphQL equivalent)
  - Cart mutations if WooGraphQL cart is not set up

  WC REST API auth (server-side only — never expose keys in browser):
    const WC_AUTH = Buffer.from(
      `${process.env.WC_CONSUMER_KEY}:${process.env.WC_CONSUMER_SECRET}`
    ).toString("base64");

    fetch(`${process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_WP_URL}/wp-json/wc/v3/products`, {
      headers: { Authorization: `Basic ${WC_AUTH}` },
    });

  This code goes in src/lib/woocommerce.ts — server-only file.

## H6. GraphQL Call Rules — How and When to Call

  GOLDEN RULE: 1 page = 1 GraphQL call. Components never fetch — they only receive props.

  ── Call count per page ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  Homepage      → 1 call  (hero + featured products + announcement bar)
  Shop page     → 1 call  (products + categories together)
  Product page  → 1 call  (product + variations + gallery together)
  Cart page     → 0 server calls (Zustand client state, no GraphQL needed)
  Cart mutation → 1 call  (client component, noCache)
  Nav + Page    → 2 PARALLEL calls via Promise.all (independent concerns)

  ── Who is allowed to call GraphQL ──────────────────────────────────
  ✅ src/app/*/page.tsx          → server component, calls query function ONCE
  ✅ src/app/*/layout.tsx        → for nav/footer global data only
  ✅ src/hooks/use*.ts           → client-side mutations (cart, auth)
  ❌ src/components/**/*         → NEVER — receives props only
  ❌ Inline in JSX               → NEVER — no fetch() inside return()

  ── Base fetch function: src/lib/graphql.ts ─────────────────────────
  One function handles all calls. Accepts revalidate or noCache option.

    export async function gql<T>(
      query: string,
      variables: Record<string, unknown> = {},
      options: { revalidate?: number; noCache?: boolean } = {}
    ): Promise<T> {
      const res = await fetch(process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_GRAPHQL_URL!, {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
        body: JSON.stringify({ query, variables }),
        next: options.noCache
          ? { revalidate: 0 }                            // cart/auth — always fresh
          : { revalidate: options.revalidate ?? 3600 },  // default 1 hour ISR
      });
      const json = await res.json();
      if (json.errors) throw new Error(json.errors[0].message);
      return json.data as T;
    }

  ── Folder structure for queries ────────────────────────────────────
  src/lib/queries/
  ├── fragments.ts    ← reusable field sets (ProductCardFields, VariationFields)
  ├── homepage.ts     ← getHomepageData()
  ├── shop.ts         ← getShopPageData()
  ├── product.ts      ← getProductBySlug(slug)
  ├── global.ts       ← getGlobalNav(), getFooter() — site-wide
  └── cart.ts         ← ADD_TO_CART, UPDATE_CART mutations

  Rule: every exported function in queries/ wraps gql() with a named query string.
        No raw gql() calls anywhere except inside queries/ files.

  ── Fragments: avoid duplicating fields ─────────────────────────────
  PRODUCT_CARD_FIELDS  → used in homepage + shop (same product card fields)
  VARIATION_FIELDS     → used in product page + cart
  Define once in fragments.ts, import with template literal: `${PRODUCT_CARD_FIELDS}`

  ── Cache values by data type ────────────────────────────────────────
  { revalidate: 86400 }  → 24 hours  — homepage sections, global nav/footer
  { revalidate: 3600  }  → 1 hour    — shop listing, product detail, categories
  { noCache: true     }  → no cache  — cart mutations, auth, coupon apply

  ── Parallel calls: only when truly independent ──────────────────────
  const [product, nav] = await Promise.all([
    getProductBySlug(slug),   // product data
    getGlobalNav(),           // separate cache, separate concern
  ]);
  Never await one then the other if they don't depend on each other — waterfall = slow.

  ── Page file pattern (always thin) ──────────────────────────────────
  // src/app/shop/page.tsx
  import { getShopPageData } from "@/lib/queries/shop"
  import ProductGrid from "@/components/shop/ProductGrid"
  import CategoryFilter from "@/components/shop/CategoryFilter"

  export default async function ShopPage() {
    const data = await getShopPageData()           // ← 1 call, all data
    return (
      <>
        <CategoryFilter categories={data.productCategories.nodes} />
        <ProductGrid products={data.products.nodes} />
      </>
    )
  }
  // No logic here. No second fetch. Components get props, nothing else.

## H6b. Key GraphQL Queries to Write (in src/lib/queries/)

  FILE: src/lib/queries/products.ts
  - GET_PRODUCTS          → shop page listing (id, slug, name, price, image, categories)
  - GET_PRODUCT_BY_SLUG   → product detail page (+ variations, gallery, description)
  - GET_PRODUCT_CATEGORIES → category filter list

  FILE: src/lib/queries/homepage.ts
  - GET_HOMEPAGE_DATA     → featured products, hero content (from WP page/ACF)

  FILE: src/lib/queries/cart.ts  (if using WooGraphQL cart)
  - ADD_TO_CART, UPDATE_CART, REMOVE_FROM_CART, GET_CART

  Rule: all GraphQL query strings live in src/lib/queries/ — never inline in a component or page.

## H6b. Dynamic Content Rule — Nothing is Static in Next.js

  RULE: Every piece of content visible on the site must come from WordPress admin.
        Zero hardcoded text, images, or data in Next.js component or page files.

  WHAT THIS MEANS:
  - Hero headline, subtext, button label        → ACF/SCF field on the Homepage
  - Section titles, body copy, images           → ACF/SCF field on each page
  - Nav links, footer links, footer text        → WordPress Menu or ACF field
  - Announcement bar text                       → ACF/SCF field (admin changes it anytime)
  - Product data (name, price, image, desc)     → WooCommerce via WPGraphQL (auto)
  - Trust badges, feature callouts, icons text  → ACF/SCF field
  - FAQ content, Education section              → ACF/SCF field or WP posts

  HOW:
  - Install plugin: "Secure Custom Fields" (SCF — free ACF fork) on WordPress
  - Install plugin: "WPGraphQL for ACF" → exposes all SCF/ACF fields to GraphQL schema
  - Create field groups in WP Admin → SCF → Field Groups, assign to Pages/Options
  - For site-wide fields (nav, footer, announcement bar) → use ACF Options Page

  REQUIRED WORDPRESS PLUGINS (add to H3 list):
  ┌──────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Secure Custom Fields (SCF)   │ Custom fields on any page/post/option         │
  │ WPGraphQL for ACF            │ Exposes SCF/ACF fields in the GraphQL schema  │
  │ ACF Options Page (built-in)  │ Site-wide fields (nav, footer, announcement)  │
  └──────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  GRAPHQL QUERY PATTERN for page content:
    query GetHomepage {
      page(id: "home", idType: URI) {
        homepage {                        # ← SCF field group name
          heroHeadline
          heroSubtext
          heroCtaLabel
          heroCtaUrl
          heroImage { sourceUrl altText }
          announcementBarText
          featuredProducts { ... }
        }
      }
    }

  NAMING CONVENTION for SCF field groups:
  - Group name = page/section (e.g. "Homepage", "Shop Page", "Footer", "Global")
  - Field names = camelCase (heroHeadline, not hero_headline)
    → WPGraphQL exposes them in camelCase automatically

  NEVER DO THIS in any Next.js file:
    <h1>Made in USA. 99% Purity. Lab Verified.</h1>   ← hardcoded = wrong
    const navLinks = ["Shop", "COAs", "Research"]      ← hardcoded = wrong
    src="/images/hero-banner.jpg"                       ← hardcoded = wrong

  ALWAYS DO THIS:
    <h1>{data.homepage.heroHeadline}</h1>              ← from GraphQL
    {menuItems.map(item => <NavLink ... />)}            ← from WP Menu via GraphQL
    <Image src={data.homepage.heroImage.sourceUrl} />  ← from WP Media via GraphQL

## H7. Confirming the setup works (first thing to do before any component work)

  STEP 1: Visit http://localhost.elixerpeptides-backend.com/graphql in browser
    → Should return: {"data":null,"errors":[{"message":"GraphQL Request must include..."}]}
    → That JSON response = WPGraphQL is active ✓

  STEP 2: Test a basic product query (use GraphiQL or Postman):
    POST http://localhost.elixerpeptides-backend.com/graphql
    Body: { "query": "{ products(first: 3) { nodes { id name slug } } }" }
    → Returns 3 products = WooGraphQL is active ✓

  STEP 3: If either fails → install the missing plugin before writing any Next.js code

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION A: PROJECT ARCHITECTURE RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## A1. Backend stays WordPress — never move logic to Next.js
- WordPress + WooCommerce = data, pricing, order creation, plugins
- Next.js = display only (catalog, PDP, cart UI, checkout trigger)
- Never replicate server-side plugin logic in Next.js (pricing, points calc, etc.)

## A2. Tagada is the checkout and pricing authority
- Checkout = redirect to Tagada hosted page (never build a custom checkout form)
- Never send price/name/image to Tagada — it reads from its own catalog
- Always carry `variation_id` (not `product_id`) on every cart line
- Tagada checkout trigger lives in `src/lib/tagada.ts` only

## A3. Cart to Tagada handoff — one-way, fire-once
- Cart → Tagada is a snapshot at checkout click. No callback, no sync back.
- Build cart payload: `[{ externalVariantId: variation_id, quantity }]` + `discountCodes` + `customer` + tracking metadata
- On return `?tgd_success=1` → clear the Zustand cart

## A4. Return page = one contract for all tracking
- One shared return page handles: Attentive, Pixel/GA4, Omnisend, Loyalty points
- Carry: `orderId`, `orderTotal`, `email`, `phone`, line items, `transaction_id`
- Fire `purchase` event from ONE source only (prefer Tagada server-side) — never fire it twice

## A5. Auth needed for wholesale prices and loyalty
- Implement JWT auth so Next.js session maps to WP user/email
- Without auth: wholesale prices and loyalty points will attribute wrongly
- Guest orders link back by billing email (handled server-side already)

## A6. Bundle product model — never show a free quantity field
- All products are variable; `pa_mg` attribute = 1 / 2 / 3-vial tiers
- Cart must show a tier stepper (swap between variations), NOT a free qty input
- Stepper logic: remove current line → add adjacent variation line

## A7. WooCommerce loyalty coupon must apply before checkout
- Apply loyalty reward coupon through Woo cart (so `woocommerce_applied_coupon` fires)
- This triggers the Elixser Rewards mirror to Tagada promotions automatically
- Pass the coupon in `discountCodes` array in the Tagada payload

## A8. No custom REST endpoints without documenting them
- Any new WP custom REST endpoint (`register_rest_route`) → document it in CLAUDE.md §7
- These live in the Elixser Rewards Custom plugin on the WordPress side

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION B: CODE QUALITY RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## B1. File size limit — 200 lines max
- Hard limit: 200 lines per file. Over 200 = split the file.
- Split by extracting: sub-components into their own files, helpers into lib/, hooks into hooks/
- Exception: auto-generated files (next.config.ts, etc.)

## B2. Component-first — no copy-paste markup
- Every repeating UI piece = its own component file
- One component per file. File name = component name (PascalCase)
- Even a small reusable button with special logic = its own file in src/components/ui/

## B3. Path comment — first line of every file
- Line 1 of every file must be a comment showing its own path (relative to src/)
- This lets any developer instantly know where the file lives

  EXAMPLES:
    // src/components/shop/ProductCard.tsx
    // src/lib/woocommerce.ts
    // src/store/cart.ts
    // src/hooks/useCart.ts

## B4. Comments — simple English, one line, WHY not WHAT
- Write a comment only when the WHY is not obvious from the code
- Use plain simple English — any developer must understand it
- Maximum one short line per comment — no paragraph blocks
- Never describe what the code does (code shows that). Describe why it does it.

  GOOD: // Tagada needs variation_id, not product_id — always send variation for variable products
  GOOD: // WC returns flat list; grouped here so category filter UI works
  BAD:  // This function maps over the products array and returns JSX for each product
  BAD:  // set loading to true  (self-explanatory — no comment needed)

## B5. Folder structure — never mix concerns

  src/
  ├── app/          → routes only (page.tsx, layout.tsx, loading.tsx, error.tsx)
  ├── components/   → UI components, grouped by feature
  │   ├── layout/   → Header, Footer, Nav
  │   ├── shop/     → ProductCard, ProductGrid, CategoryFilter
  │   ├── cart/     → CartItem, CartSummary, CartDrawer
  │   ├── product/  → ProductImages, ProductInfo, BundleStepper
  │   └── ui/       → Button, Badge, Spinner (generic reusables)
  ├── lib/          → API clients and pure utility functions
  ├── hooks/        → custom React hooks (useCart, useProduct, etc.)
  ├── store/        → Zustand stores only
  └── types/        → TypeScript interfaces only

  RULES:
  - app/        → calls components. NO inline JSX logic.
  - lib/        → pure functions. NO React, NO hooks, NO JSX.
  - components/ → receives props. NO direct fetch() calls.
  - hooks/      → state and side effects. Calls lib/ for data.

## B6. Naming conventions

  Component file   → PascalCase          ProductCard.tsx
  Hook file        → use + camelCase     useCart.ts
  Lib/util file    → camelCase           woocommerce.ts, formatPrice.ts
  Store file       → camelCase           cart.ts
  Type/Interface   → PascalCase          Product, CartItem, WCVariation
  Constant         → UPPER_SNAKE_CASE    WC_API_URL, REVALIDATE_SHOP
  CSS              → Tailwind utilities only, no custom class names

## B7. Page files must be thin — no logic inside
- page.tsx = fetch data (if needed) + pass to component. Nothing else.
- No business logic, no long JSX trees, no inline styles in page files.

  GOOD:
    // src/app/shop/page.tsx
    import ProductGrid from "@/components/shop/ProductGrid"
    import { getProducts } from "@/lib/woocommerce"
    export default async function ShopPage() {
      const products = await getProducts()
      return <ProductGrid products={products} />
    }

## B8. TypeScript — no `any`, ever
- All API response types in src/types/
- Write the interface first, then write the function
- If type is unknown: use `unknown` and narrow it — never `any`

## B9. Server Components by default — `"use client"` only when required
- Default: Server Component (no directive needed)
- Add "use client" ONLY when the component uses: useState, useEffect, event handlers, Zustand
- Cart, interactive buttons, drawers = client components
- Product pages, shop listing, homepage sections = server components (faster load)

## B10. Environment variables — two strict categories

  NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix  → visible in browser + server  → safe to expose (site URL, WP URL)
  no prefix            → server only                   → secrets (WC keys, tokens)

  NEVER use a secret key inside a Client Component ("use client" file)
  API calls with secret keys → lib/ functions → called from Server Components or Route Handlers only

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION C: ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH (mandatory)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## C1. Each concern has exactly one home

  WooCommerce API calls    → src/lib/woocommerce.ts       (only file that calls WC REST)
  WordPress REST calls     → src/lib/wordpress.ts         (menus, pages, homepage content)
  Tagada checkout trigger  → src/lib/tagada.ts            (INIT_CHECKOUT payload builder)
  Cart state               → src/store/cart.ts            (Zustand, localStorage persist)
  Price formatting         → src/lib/formatPrice.ts       (currency display logic)
  Attentive globals        → src/lib/attentive.ts         (attn_cart, attn_product, attn_order)
  Auth/session             → src/lib/auth.ts              (JWT → WP user resolve)

## C2. No scattered fetch() calls
- If two places need the same data → extract to lib/ and import
- Never duplicate an API call across components or pages

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION D: PERFORMANCE RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## D1. ISR (Incremental Static Regeneration) for catalog pages
- Shop listing page    → revalidate: 3600   (rebuild every 1 hour)
- Product detail page  → revalidate: 3600
- Homepage sections    → revalidate: 86400  (rebuild every 24 hours)
- Cart / Checkout      → no cache, "use client", real-time only

## D2. Images — next/image only, never <img>
- Every image = <Image> from "next/image"
- Add WordPress domain to next.config.ts images.remotePatterns
- Always set width + height (or fill) to avoid layout shift

## D3. Fonts — next/font only, never a <link> tag
- Load all fonts via next/font/google (or next/font/local)
- No Google Fonts <link> in layout.tsx or _document
- This eliminates font-related layout shift and speeds up LCP

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION I: LOADING STATES & SKELETONS (MANDATORY — never ship a page without one)
# Rule: a click must NEVER look ignored. The page changes at once, and grey
#       skeleton holds the layout until the real data arrives.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## I1. The contract — this is not optional

  User clicks ANY link
      → the new page opens IMMEDIATELY (never wait on the old page)
      → its skeleton shows while the data loads
      → the real content replaces the skeleton in the SAME layout

  Never allowed:
  - A blank white screen while data loads
  - The old page frozen on screen after a click
  - A bare spinner in the middle of an empty page
  - An empty div used as a placeholder (e.g. <div className="min-h-[380px]" />)
  - "No products found" / "Your cart is empty" shown BEFORE the data has loaded

## I2. Every route needs a loading.tsx — no exceptions

  src/app/<route>/loading.tsx  → renders that route's skeleton component

  A route is NOT done until its loading.tsx exists. This is part of the task,
  not a polish step for later. Add it in the SAME commit as the page.

  Already built (keep them in sync when a page changes):
    src/app/shop/loading.tsx            → ShopSkeleton
    src/app/product/[slug]/loading.tsx  → ProductSkeleton
    src/app/cart/loading.tsx            → CartHero + CartPageSkeleton

  Still to add when those pages start fetching real data:
    src/app/loading.tsx        (homepage — after P1-16)
    src/app/coas/loading.tsx
    src/app/checkout/loading.tsx
    src/app/account/**/loading.tsx

## I3. Client-side waits need a skeleton too

  loading.tsx only covers server navigation. ALSO show a skeleton when:
  - Reading the cart from localStorage (first client render)
  - A fetch inside a hook is still running (totals, FBT suggestions, search)
  - A "use client" component has data that is not ready yet

  Pattern:
    const hasMounted = useHasMounted();
    if (!hasMounted) return <XyzSkeleton />;   // never an empty div

## I4. Skeleton must MATCH the real layout, pixel for pixel

- Copy the real component's wrapper classes: grid columns, gaps, padding, min-height
- Same number of placeholder rows/cards as the page normally shows
- Nothing may jump or resize when real data lands (zero layout shift)
- Vary the bar widths (w-20 / w-24 / w-16) so it reads like text, not a barcode

## I5. Build skeletons from the shared primitives — never hand-roll

  src/components/ui/Skeleton.tsx   → one grey shimmering block (size via className)
  src/components/ui/Spinner.tsx    → small ring, ONLY inside buttons

  Rules:
  - Never write a raw animated grey <div>; always use <Skeleton />
  - One skeleton file per component, named <Component>Skeleton.tsx,
    living next to the component it mirrors
  - Skeleton files are Server Components — no "use client", no props, no logic

## I6. Buttons that start work must say so

- A button that triggers a request switches to a pending label + <Spinner />
  (e.g. "Apply coupon" → "Checking…", "Update Cart" → "Updating…")
- Disable it while pending so it cannot be double-clicked
- NEVER let a pending state change an element's width in the header or nav —
  that makes the menu twitch. Colour/label only, or position the spinner absolutely.

## I7. Accessibility — a skeleton is not just a picture

- Wrap each skeleton block in role="status" + aria-label="Loading <what>"
- Put aria-busy={isLoading} on a box whose numbers are being re-checked
- Every <Skeleton /> is aria-hidden already — do not announce each bar
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion: the [data-skeleton] rule in globals.css stops
  the shimmer, so never add a custom animation that ignores it

## I8. Never fake slowness to "show" the skeleton

- No artificial setTimeout, no disabling prefetch, no removing ISR caching
- A fast page that skips the skeleton is CORRECT behaviour, not a bug
- To test one: DevTools → Network → Slow 4G, or clear .next/cache and cold-load

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION E: WHAT NOT TO BUILD IN NEXT.JS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## E1. Do NOT rebuild these — they run server-side and stay in WordPress
- Points earning / expiry calculation (WPLoyalty)
- Referral CPA tracking (Elixser Rewards v0.1/v0.2)
- Coupon → Tagada promotion mirror (Elixser Rewards v0.3)
- Guest order → account linking (Elixser Rewards v0.4)
- WPC quantity pricing calculation
- Wholesale role price calculation
- Discount Rules cart calculation
- Order creation / payment processing (Tagada handles this)

## E2. These WordPress plugin FRONTENDS are dead — rebuild them in Next.js
- WPLoyalty customer widget (points, rewards, referral URL display)
- Elixser bundle stepper (1/2/3 vial tier swap)
- Cart Abandonment Recovery UI (likely cut — Tagada does this)
- Wholesale registration form
- WiserReview widget per product page
- Age / RUO gate (was a theme template + jQuery + admin-ajax — now Section J)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECTION J: AGE / RUO GATE  ✅ BUILT
# Ported from the WordPress theme (age-gate.php / .css / .js + kg_age_gate_* in functions.php).
# Rule: the design is fixed — match age-gate.css value for value, never redesign it.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

## J1. What it does

  A guest sees a full-screen lockout before anything else on the site. They give an
  email, pick a field of research, and tick both acknowledgements (21+, research use
  only). That creates a WooCommerce customer, signs them in, and the gate disappears.

  There is NO close button and Escape does nothing. "Exit" leaves for google.com.

## J2. Files — one job each

  src/app/layout.tsx                  → reads the cookie, renders the gate for guests only
  src/components/gate/AgeGate.tsx     → full-screen overlay, scroll lock, path check
  src/components/gate/GateCard.tsx    → the dark panel: eyebrow, title, intro, footnote
  src/components/gate/GateForm.tsx    → all form state and the submit
  src/components/gate/GateField.tsx   → labelled control shell + shared control classes
  src/components/gate/GateSelect.tsx  → research dropdown with its own chevron
  src/components/gate/GateCheck.tsx   → acknowledgement row with the drawn tick
  src/components/gate/GateSubmit.tsx  → the Confirm & enter button
  src/components/gate/GateError.tsx   → red message, plus a sign-in link when taken
  src/actions/ageGate.ts              → validates, registers, saves the session cookie
  src/lib/ageGateCopy.ts              → every word, the research list, the exit URL
  src/types/gate.ts                   → GateState

## J3. Rules that must not be broken

  - Who sees it is decided on the SERVER (layout reads the elx_session cookie). Never
    move that to the client — the page would flash before the gate landed.
  - /account stays open while gated, so somebody who already has a login can sign in.
    GATE_OPEN_PATH in ageGateCopy.ts is the only place that path is written.
  - RESEARCH_FIELDS values must stay in step with kg_get_research_field_options() in
    the WordPress theme, and the meta key stays `field_of_research`. WordPress reads
    both back on the user profile screen.
  - The visitor never picks a password. One is generated and thrown away; they come
    back through the magic link or a password reset.
  - Do NOT hand the server action to <form action={...}>. React 19 blanks the fields
    after every reply, which would wipe what the visitor typed when an email is taken.
    Submit through startTransition instead (see GateForm).
  - Gate colours live in globals.css as --color-gate-* tokens. No hex in a component.

## J4. Pixel match — verified element by element against the live site

  Every element's y-position and height was measured on elixserpeptides.com at
  1440x900 and matches to 0.0px, card height included. Re-measure the same way
  before claiming any change still matches.

  Four traps that cost real time, because the live theme inherits a FIXED
  line-height of 22.4px while globals.css uses a unitless 1.6:

    eyebrow     needs leading-[22.4px]   (1.6 x 11px = 17.6 is 4.8px too short)
    footnote    needs leading-[22.4px]   (1.6 x 10px = 16 is 6.4px too short)
    field label needs leading-none       (live computes to exactly 13px)
    button      needs leading-[normal]   NOT leading-normal — Tailwind's
                                         leading-normal is 1.5, the CSS keyword
                                         `normal` is ~1.2, a 4px difference

  Two more, carried over from the live site on purpose:

    - The title carries mt-2. The WP theme gives h2 an 8px margin-top that
      age-gate.css never resets; Tailwind preflight does, so it is added back.
    - The email box is SHORTER and greyer than the dropdown (padding 8px, radius
      3px, border #666) because the theme's own input rule beats age-gate.css
      there. GATE_INPUT copies it on purpose; GATE_SELECT keeps the real
      age-gate.css look. If the client ever wants the two to match, point
      GATE_INPUT at GATE_SELECT's values — one line.

## J5. Known trade-off

  Reading the cookie in the root layout turns every route from Static into Dynamic
  (`ƒ` in the build output) — /shop, /coas and /research used to be prerendered.
  Measured on a production build with a cold cache: the first request to /shop takes
  ~3.9s and calls WordPress once, every request after it answers in ~30ms with no
  WordPress call at all, until the hour is up. Site chrome (getGlobalData) is on
  unstable_cache and asks WordPress once a week. So WordPress load is unchanged;
  only the React render repeats. A gated site cannot be served statically anyway.

